    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> wrote it in 1377 as the introduction to a universal history, and it came to be regarded as an independent work in his own lifetime.</li><li>It sets out a new science of human society, ʿilm al-ʿumrān, and explains the rise and fall of dynasties through ʿasabiyyah, or group feeling.</li><li>It was received without much enthusiasm for centuries, first printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1857, and translated into French in 1863 and English in 1958.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> wrote that when officials were questioned about armies, or the goods of the wealthy were assessed, the true figures would be found to amount to a tenth of what those people had said.</p></aside>
    </section>

